    {"t": "h2", "text": "Signal quality"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Quality is more than a cosmetic badge. It influences the aggregation of windows, whether a class is shown at all, whether a baseline can use a record, and how strongly a window contributes to a multi-window result. A recording shorter than 20 seconds loses 45 points; missing cardiac loses 20; missing EDA loses 12; a flat channel loses 18; heavy high-frequency ECG noise loses 14\u201324; an implausible heart rate loses 20; irregular beats lose 14; out-of-range EDA or temperature loses 8\u201310. The result is graded good (\u226580), fair (\u226560), poor (\u226540) or insufficient (<40)."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "This is a rule-based quality layer, not a validated artefact detector. It can miss noise that looks physiological and can penalise unusual but genuine signals. It does, however, prevent the most obvious failure mode: silently reporting a confident class from a disconnected or extremely short recording."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "Probability calibration: the most important scientific gap"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The interface calls the winning softmax probability \u201cconfidence\u201d. That is legitimate as a model score, but it is not a calibrated probability. In this installation a cached WESAD neutral window produced confidence 1.000 and a 48-second upload produced 0.9936, both rounded high values. Modern boosted models are often overconfident, and no isotonic/Platt calibration or reliability diagram is computed on held-out participants. A faculty examiner is likely to ask this; the honest answer is that calibration is future work."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Entropy uncertainty is also not a calibrated interval. It measures how spread the three class probabilities are. It is a useful relative signal, not a statement such as \u201c95% chance the true class is within this range\u201d."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "What is scientifically justified today"},
    {"t": "b", "items": [
        "Extracting standard time-domain cardiac, electrodermal, respiratory, temperature and motion features from windowed signals.",
        "Comparing three established classifiers with participant-independent grouped cross-validation.",
        "Reporting balanced accuracy alongside accuracy for an imbalanced three-class problem.",
        "Using permutation importance on held-out participants for global model behaviour.",
        "Presenting predictions as pattern similarity and explicitly refusing diagnosis.",
        "Showing quality and provenance next to every estimate.",
        "Labelling synthetic data and cached reference data rather than passing them off as live measurements.",
    ]},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "What requires further work"},
    {"t": "b", "items": [
        "External validation on a second dataset, consumer device and real-world population.",
        "Probability calibration and reliability curves with participant-independent calibration folds.",
        "Chest-to-wrist transfer validation before accepting wrist PPG uploads as comparable.",
        "Unit detection/conversion and device-specific preprocessing.",
        "A resting-state protocol for baseline collection and a longitudinal evaluation of whether personalisation helps.",
        "Fairness analysis across age, sex, skin tone and health conditions; WESAD does not support this adequately.",
        "Clinical or regulatory review if any health claim is ever made.",
    ]},

